      "text": "To find the product of the first two terms of the arithmetic sequence, we need to determine the first term of the sequence. We are given that the difference between two consecutive terms is 1 and the fifth term is 11.\n\nLet's denote the first term of the sequence as \\(a_1\\) and the common difference as \\(d\\). We know from the problem statement that \\(d = 1\\).\n\nThe eighth term (\\(a_8\\)) can be expressed as:\n\\[ a_8 = a_1 + 7d \\]\n\nWe are given that the fifth term (\\(a_5\\)) is 11:\n\\[ a_5 = a_1 + 4d = 11 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(d = 1\\) into the equation for the fifth term:\n\\[ a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1 = 11 \\]\n\\[ a_1 + 4 = 11 \\]\n\\[ a_1 = 11 - 4 \\]\n\\[ a_1 = 7 \\]\n\nNow we know the first term \\(a_1 = 7\\). To find the second term \\(a_2\\):\n\\[ a_2 = a_1 + d \\]\n\\[ a_2 = 7 + 1 \\]\n\\[ a_2 = 8 \\]\n\nWe need the product of the first two terms, \\(a_1 \\times a_2\\):\n\\[ \\text{Product} = 7 \\times 8 = 56 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the first two terms of the arithmetic sequence is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
