      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $a$ be the first term and $d$ be the common difference of the arithmetic sequence. Since $d=1$, the common difference between consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe $\\textit{n}$ th term of an arithmetic sequence can be represented as:\n\\begin{align}\n    a + (n-1)d&=a + (n-1)(1) \\\\\n    &= a + n - 1\n\\end{align}\n\nThe fifth term, $t_5$, is given as 11. Therefore,\n\\begin{align}\n    a + 4(d) &= 11 \\\\\n    a + 4 &= 11 \\\\\n    \\text{Set } (n &\\rightarrow 1) \\\\\n    a + d &= 11 \\\\\n    a + 1 &= 11 \\\\\n    \\text{Set } (n &\\rightarrow 2) \\\\\n    a + 2d &= 11 \\\\\n\\end{align}\nEnding up with a system of equations:\n\\begin{align}\n    a + 1 &= 11 \\\\\n    a + 2d &= 11\n\\end{align}\n\nThe difference equations are already solved for us. Let's begin solving the simultaneous equations using substitution, elimination, or cross multiplication. By cross multiplication or cross-simplification:\n\\begin{align}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left(11 + 11 \\times 2\\right) \\\\\n= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 22 \\\\\n= 11\n\\end{align}\n\nTo find $a$, multiply both sides of the equation by $(1/2)$:\n\\begin{align}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 11 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 11 d \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 11 &= \\frac{1 + d}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{11}{2} &= \\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{d}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{11}{2} &= \\frac{d}{2} \\\\\n0 &= d \\\\\n\\end{align}\n\nThe common difference is 0, implying the answer is the product of the first two terms, which are $a$ and $a+1$:\n\\begin{align}\n\\textrm{Product} & = a \\times \\left(a + 1\\right) \\\\\n& = 11 \\times 12 \\\\\n& = 132\n\\end{align} The answer is: 132",
